module product.rack_mount


// SPATIAL-4B: RACK-UNIT OCCUPANCY OVER CITED HARDWARE. A chassis from extdeps.chassis occupying
// rack units in a rack from extdeps.rack. Every dimension below is either a cited vendor fact or
// derived from EIA-310 through std.measure's micrometers_per_rack_unit; none is authored here,
// which is the point of doing this band against real catalogue rows rather than invented ones.
//
// THE SCOPE IS THE RACK, NOT THE ROOM. This module models no relationship between a rack and any
// space, and imports no geometry. The room-fit relation an earlier revision carried is deleted
// rather than weakened. A reader looking here for "which room does this rack stand in" should find
// nothing, because the answer requires a cited external rack height the corpus does not have.
//
// The reason it is deleted rather than patched: rack_units x 44.45 mm is the MOUNTING APERTURE —
// the usable vertical space between the rails. It is not the rack's external envelope: frames,
// bases, top members, feet, casters and construction clearances all extend past the aperture, and
// RackCatalogRow carries no external height for any of them. The earlier RoomMountedRack compared
// the aperture against a room's ceiling and called the result a fit, which moved this band from an
// inexact answer to an EXACT answer to the wrong physical question — a plausible-but-false
// admission, which is worse than the truncation it replaced because nothing about it looks
// approximate.
//
// It also bypassed the join SPATIAL-4A had just established: it took a bare EnclosedSpace as "the
// room", so it could not say WHICH room the rack stood in, could not distinguish two geometrically
// identical rooms, and did not require the geometry to belong to any admitted world — while the
// type name asserted a mounting relation that one scalar comparison does not establish.
//
// Dissolve-on, and it is one condition rather than two: a cited external height on the rack catalog
// authority. When it lands, the relation returns taking a World and a RoomIdentity, resolving the
// room through the world and retaining that identity in the result, and its discriminating control
// is the case the deleted version got wrong — a ceiling above the aperture but below the external
// height must REFUSE.
//
// A MOUNT IS AN INTERVAL, NOT A POSITION. A chassis does not sit "at U5", it occupies U5 through U8
// inclusive, and modelling it as a single position is what makes two mounts collide silently: the
// positions differ, so nothing looks wrong, while the metal overlaps. The interval is DERIVED from
// the cited chassis height and the lowest unit, so it cannot disagree with the hardware it
// describes.
//
// WHAT IS NOT DECIDABLE FROM THE CITED ROWS, stated rather than guessed. ChassisCatalogRow carries
// motherboard_support, but BaseboardCatalogRow carries no form factor, so whether a given board
// FITS a given chassis cannot be answered from the corpus today. Authoring the ALTRAD8UD's form
// factor here to make the check work would be inventing a vendor fact - the one thing extdeps
// exists to prevent - so the check is absent, not approximated, and a caller asking for it gets
// nothing rather than a plausible answer. Dissolve-on: BaseboardCatalogRow gains a cited
// form_factor from the ASRock product page already anchored in extdeps.boards.asrock_rack, at
// which point board admission becomes a wall here.
//
// Depth and width are likewise absent from both cited rows, so the only spatial quantity here is
// vertical, and even that is the mounting APERTURE rather than any envelope the rack occupies.
// External dimensions arrive when a cited row carries them.

// Every quantity in a refusal carries its carrier rather than its unit in a field NAME. An earlier
// revision of this sum spelled its heights with _um suffixes on bare Int - the "unit in the name,
// scalar in the type" pattern std.measure exists to remove, on the refusal path of the very change
// that added the Micrometer carrier to stop truncating. Positions take product.placement_supply's
// RackUnitPosition and capacities take std.measure's RackUnit: a position is an ordinal slot index
// and a capacity is a cardinal magnitude, a distinction placement_supply already draws and this
// module must not re-draw.
// The two identities on MountsCollide are named for their PHYSICAL relationship and derived from
// lowest_unit. An earlier revision passed them as (already-admitted, incoming), which is an
// input-order fact wearing position names: a mount admitted at U10 colliding with an incoming one
// at U8 reported U10 as the lower.
// OccupancyExceedsCapacity is its own arm, because reusing MountExceedsRackHeight would have put a
// total occupied CARDINALITY in a field that carries a mount's top POSITION everywhere else — one
// field meaning two things, so a fired diagnostic would mislabel the failure it exists to explain.
// This arm is unreachable while admission holds; it is the typed answer if the invariant behind
// rack_units_free is ever broken, not a case a caller is expected to hit.
type MountRefusal
  = MountBelowFirstUnit { lowest_unit: product.placement_supply.RackUnitPosition }
  | MountExceedsRackHeight { top_unit: product.placement_supply.RackUnitPosition, rack_capacity: std.measure.RackUnit }
  | MountsCollide { lower: product.placement_supply.PhysicalAssetIdentity, upper: product.placement_supply.PhysicalAssetIdentity }
  | AssetMountedTwice { asset: product.placement_supply.PhysicalAssetIdentity }
  | OccupancyExceedsCapacity { occupied: std.measure.RackUnit, capacity: std.measure.RackUnit }

type MountConstruction<T>
  = MountReady { value: T }
  | MountRefused { cause: MountRefusal }

// The occupied interval is derived, so it is a function rather than a stored pair: storing top
// beside lowest would let an edit to the chassis leave the top describing a height the hardware
// does not have.
type ChassisMount {
  asset: product.placement_supply.PhysicalAssetIdentity
  chassis: extdeps.chassis.types.ChassisCatalogRow
  lowest_unit: product.placement_supply.RackUnitPosition
}

// The span IS the cited chassis row's height - a cardinal magnitude, so it stays a RackUnit and is
// not re-derived. The top unit is the one place ordinal and cardinal meet: position + magnitude - 1
// is affine arithmetic, and it is the only site that reads the magnitude's count, deliberately, so
// the crossing happens once and is visible rather than spread across the occupancy surface.
fn mount_unit_span(m: ChassisMount) -> std.measure.RackUnit { m.chassis.rack_units }

fn mount_top_unit(m: ChassisMount) -> product.placement_supply.RackUnitPosition {
  m.lowest_unit + std.measure.rack_unit_count(u: mount_unit_span(m: m)) - 1
}

// Rack units are numbered from 1 at the bottom, so a mount occupies lowest .. lowest + span - 1
// inclusive. Two mounts collide when those closed intervals intersect at all - unlike the spatial
// bands, where touching is adjacency, a shared rack unit is two chassis in one hole.
fn mounts_collide(a: ChassisMount, b: ChassisMount) -> Bool {
  a.lowest_unit <= mount_top_unit(m: b) && b.lowest_unit <= mount_top_unit(m: a)
}

fn asset_eq(a: product.placement_supply.PhysicalAssetIdentity, b: product.placement_supply.PhysicalAssetIdentity) -> Bool {
  (a as String) == (b as String)
}

type MountAdmission {
  admitted: List<ChassisMount>
  refusal: MountRefusal?
}

fn admit_mount(acc: MountAdmission, rack: extdeps.rack.types.RackCatalogRow, m: ChassisMount) -> MountAdmission {
  match acc.refusal {
    v2.std.optional.Present { value: r } => MountAdmission { admitted: acc.admitted, refusal: v2.std.optional.Present { value: r } }
    v2.std.optional.Absent =>
      if m.lowest_unit < 1 {
        MountAdmission { admitted: acc.admitted, refusal: v2.std.optional.Present { value: MountBelowFirstUnit { lowest_unit: m.lowest_unit } } }
      } else if mount_top_unit(m: m) > std.measure.rack_unit_count(u: rack.rack_units) {
        MountAdmission {
          admitted: acc.admitted,
          refusal: v2.std.optional.Present { value: MountExceedsRackHeight { top_unit: mount_top_unit(m: m), rack_capacity: rack.rack_units } },
        }
      } else {
        let conflict = fold(acc.admitted, init: none, f: (found, other) =>
          match found {
            v2.std.optional.Present { value: r } => v2.std.optional.Present { value: r }
            v2.std.optional.Absent =>
              if asset_eq(a: other.asset, b: m.asset) {
                v2.std.optional.Present { value: AssetMountedTwice { asset: m.asset } }
              } else if mounts_collide(a: other, b: m) {
                v2.std.optional.Present {
                  value: if other.lowest_unit <= m.lowest_unit {
                    MountsCollide { lower: other.asset, upper: m.asset }
                  } else {
                    MountsCollide { lower: m.asset, upper: other.asset }
                  }
                }
              } else {
                none
              }
          })
        match conflict {
          v2.std.optional.Present { value: r } => MountAdmission { admitted: acc.admitted, refusal: v2.std.optional.Present { value: r } }
          v2.std.optional.Absent => MountAdmission { admitted: concat(acc.admitted, [m]), refusal: none }
        }
      }
  }
}

type RackInstance sole_constructor {
  identity: product.placement_supply.RackIdentity
  catalog: extdeps.rack.types.RackCatalogRow
  mounts: List<ChassisMount>
}

fn rack_instance(identity: product.placement_supply.RackIdentity, catalog: extdeps.rack.types.RackCatalogRow, mounts: List<ChassisMount>) -> MountConstruction<RackInstance> {
  let admitted = fold(mounts, init: MountAdmission { admitted: [], refusal: none }, f: (acc, m) =>
    admit_mount(acc: acc, rack: catalog, m: m))
  match admitted.refusal {
    v2.std.optional.Present { value: r } => MountRefused { cause: r }
    v2.std.optional.Absent => MountReady { value: RackInstance { identity: identity, catalog: catalog, mounts: mounts } }
  }
}

fn rack_mounts(r: RackInstance) -> List<ChassisMount> { r.mounts }

fn rack_identity(r: RackInstance) -> product.placement_supply.RackIdentity { r.identity }

fn rack_units_occupied(r: RackInstance) -> std.measure.RackUnit {
  fold(r.mounts, init: std.measure.rack_unit(0), f: (acc, m) => std.measure.measure_add(acc, mount_unit_span(m: m)))
}

// FREE CAPACITY IS A DIFFERENCE, AND std.measure DELIBERATELY HAS NO SUBTRACT: the Nat-backed
// carrier cannot represent a negative magnitude, so a general subtraction would need an underflow
// answer nobody has decided. Here it cannot underflow, and the reason is a construction rather
// than an assumption - admission refuses any mount whose top exceeds the rack, and refuses any two
// mounts that share a unit, so the admitted spans are disjoint intervals inside the capacity and
// their sum is bounded by it. The guard is kept anyway and REFUSES rather than clamping to zero,
// because a fabricated zero would report a full rack as empty; if it ever fires, the invariant
// above is broken and that is worth a typed answer rather than a plausible number.
fn rack_units_free(r: RackInstance) -> MountConstruction<std.measure.RackUnit> {
  let occupied = rack_units_occupied(r: r)
  if std.measure.measure_le(occupied, r.catalog.rack_units) {
    MountReady { value: std.measure.rack_unit(count: std.measure.rack_unit_count(u: r.catalog.rack_units) - std.measure.rack_unit_count(u: occupied)) }
  } else {
    MountRefused { cause: OccupancyExceedsCapacity { occupied: occupied, capacity: r.catalog.rack_units } }
  }
}

// HEIGHT IS CARRIED IN MICROMETRES BECAUSE A RACK UNIT IS NOT A WHOLE NUMBER OF MILLIMETRES.
// EIA-310 fixes 1U at 44.45 mm, so 18U is 800.1 mm - and an earlier revision of this function
// returned Millimeter, computing units * 44450 / 1000, which truncated that to 800 while the
// comment beside it claimed no rounding entered. A witness then asserted the truncated 800 and
// passed, which is the fabricated value with its own test pinning it in place. The micrometre is
// exact for every whole unit count, so the quantity is carried there and the caller converts only
// where it is willing to say what it wants done with the remainder.
// Named for what it measures. This is the aperture, not the rack, and no consumer here may treat
// it as an external dimension.
fn rack_units_to_micrometres(units: std.measure.RackUnit) -> std.measure.Micrometer {
  std.measure.micrometer(count: std.measure.rack_unit_count(u: units) * std.measure.micrometers_per_rack_unit())
}

// NO ROOM-FIT RELATION IN THIS INCREMENT, and the reason is worth stating because an earlier
// revision shipped one that looked right.
//
// rack_units x 44.45 mm is the MOUNTING APERTURE - the usable vertical space between the rails. It
// is not the rack's external envelope: frames, bases, top members, feet, casters and construction
// clearances all extend past the aperture, and RackCatalogRow carries no external height for any
// of them. The earlier RoomMountedRack compared the aperture against a room's ceiling and called
// the result a fit, which moved this band from an inexact answer to an EXACT answer to the wrong
// physical question - a plausible-but-false admission, which is worse than the truncation it
// replaced because nothing about it looks approximate.
//
// It also bypassed the join SPATIAL-4A had just established: it took a bare EnclosedSpace as "the
// room", so it could not say WHICH room the rack stood in, could not distinguish two geometrically
// identical rooms, and did not require the geometry to belong to any admitted world - while the
// type name asserted a mounting relation that one scalar comparison does not establish.
//
// Both are removed rather than patched. Dissolve-on, and it is one condition rather than two: a
// cited external height on the rack catalog authority. When it lands, the relation returns taking a
// World and a RoomIdentity, resolving the room through the world and retaining that identity in the
// result, and its discriminating control is the case the deleted version got wrong - a ceiling
// above the aperture but below the external height must REFUSE.
fn rack_mounting_aperture_height(r: RackInstance) -> std.measure.Micrometer {
  rack_units_to_micrometres(units: r.catalog.rack_units)
}
